<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Saturated Schnakenberg Turing wavelength for Dugesia japonica dorsal pigment at 6.912 µm (Holocene, Pass 2) (Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian))",
        "Saturated Schnakenberg Turing wavelength for Dugesia japonica dorsal pigment at 6.912 µm (Holocene, Pass 2) faithfully reproduces Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-5> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Does not claim 6.912 µm was measured on this species. It is the slot's detail-scale operating point (6.912e-6 m, log10 = -5.160), age 0 Ma.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim a fossil predecessor. Chronoseries 1/1.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim the brightness mask separates every worm on the Nogi plate.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Nonlinear radial-FFT wavelength, KS interior comparison, diffusion-ratio sweep, and equal-diffusion ablation.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Pale-body segmentation of the wild-type voucher.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: κ = 0.15, R = 48, D_u solved for λ_c = 6.912 µm, spectral integrator, seed 3213.",
        "reported_in_source: Nogi et al. (2009) published the CC BY 2.5 regenerated Dugesia japonica plate. Kalininsky's iNaturalist photo 226612281 is CC BY 4.0.",
        "reported_in_source: Schnakenberg (1979), Gierer & Meinhardt (1972), and Murray (1981) supply the kinetics and the pigment-prepattern reading.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing (1952) gives the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber.",
        "transfer_boundary: Kondo (2009) is a fish pigment wave. Lapan & Reddien (2012) is Schmidtea eye regeneration. Herath & Lobo (2020) is planarian fission, not this wavelength.",
        "transfer_boundary: Neither specimen plate is a calibrated 6.912 µm micrograph." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Dugesia japonica" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "II. Anaima (Invertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Zoophyta & Vermes (Cnidaria/Platyhelminthes)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Dugesia" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/dugesia_japonica_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian); environment: python 3.12, lean not installed in this VM; theorems are Lean 4 prelude, unchecked here; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing (1952) gives the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber. | Schnakenberg (1979), Gierer & Meinhardt (1972), and Murray (1981) supply the kinetics and the pigment-prepattern reading. | Nogi et al. (2009) published the CC BY 2.5 regenerated Dugesia japonica plate. Kalininsky's iNaturalist photo 226612281 is CC BY 4.0.; reconstructed_in_script: κ = 0.15, R = 48, D_u solved for λ_c = 6.912 µm, spectral integrator, seed 3213. | Nonlinear radial-FFT wavelength, KS interior comparison, diffusion-ratio sweep, and equal-diffusion ablation. | Pale-body segmentation of the wild-type voucher.; transfer_boundary: Kondo (2009) is a fish pigment wave. Lapan & Reddien (2012) is Schmidtea eye regeneration. Herath & Lobo (2020) is planarian fission, not this wavelength. | Neither specimen plate is a calibrated 6.912 µm micrograph.; does_not_claim: Does not claim 6.912 µm was measured on this species. It is the slot's detail-scale operating point (6.912e-6 m, log10 = -5.160), age 0 Ma. | Does not claim a fossil predecessor. Chronoseries 1/1. | Does not claim the brightness mask separates every worm on the Nogi plate.]; phenomenon_size: 8.9 µm (8.928e-06 m, log10=-5.049 m, tier=3_subcellular_um); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 8.9 µm to 2.6 cm): 8.9 µm:dugesia-japonica-bioelectric-polarity, 32 µm:dugesia-japonica-vertex-packing, 581 µm:dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength, 1.1 mm:dugesia-japonica-clock-wavefront, 1.9 mm:dugesia-japonica-morphogen-condensation, 2.4 mm:dugesia-japonica-flux-canalization, 2.6 mm:dugesia-japonica-elastic-buckling, 3.0 mm:dugesia-japonica-branching-tree" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00289234>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(79)90042-0>,
